;;; A hypothesis rule whose conclusion is an `(and …)` proposed its first conjunct
;;; alone, and the others were dropped in silence.
;;;
;;; A hypothesis is one fact, each a commitment of its own, and `h` supposes two,
;;; `(p a)` and `(q a)`, as one. The hypothesis layer read the first template of
;;; the rule's plan alone, so H was `{(p a)}` and R two states, ∅ and
;;; `{(p a)}`; `(q a)` was proposed by nothing and no line said so. The
;;; mechanisms check and the hypothesis relations read the first conjunct too
;;; (`asserted_relation`), so `q` was no relation the rule proposes.
;;;
;;; The right answer is a refusal, one line (Q-M1f.271). Written as a rule that
;;; concludes `(open (and (p a) (q a)))`, it is refused at the parse: `and`
;;; heads no fact, and `open` takes one (Q-M1f.567). Two conjuncts `(open (p a))`
;;; and `(open (q a))` give H `{(p a), (q a)}` and R four states.
;;;
;;;
(relation p Thing :why "p {?3}")
(relation q Thing :why "q {?3}")
(rule h () :match () :assert (open (and (p a) (q a))) :why "propose p and q of a")
